eparation of data entry because  I have to enter most of my own data from  the archives and separating that from  data analysis as Jenny Brian has talked  about a lot and so I was interested in  it but I didn't know what to do and a  lot of what people say when when you  know people come up and they say oh I  want to learn to code they say okay  we'll pick a project well I didn't know  what a project was okay I'll pick a  project what what's a project what  programming language am I going to need  what what do I even need I don't know  what I need so this really kind of kept  me for a long time and trying to take  what I knew from history from the  humanities in learning a whole new skill  I didn't really know what to do and so I  think that the tidy Tuesday whole  projects is really interest  in this regard because it demonstrates  okay here's something that you can do  and it's manageable and it's reasonable  and you can get it but I didn't know  that so I said okay I'm going to you  know after long period of not doing  anything I said okay I'm going to  actually put my foot down and learn this  whole digital thing so I said okay I  think I have a project that would be a  project I have in the archives 6,000  letters or over 6,000 letters that were  sent to merchants Daniel von der Molen  over a period of twenty two years from  1578 to 1600 and I said okay I could  probably map these and so I Haman HOD  and I thought about okay is there a GUI  application that I could just put some  data in and get something out and I said  no I'm going to learn how to do it by  myself I had some help from a friend and  I looked around and I said okay why not  this whole our thing so how then did I  learn how to program in our basically  well how did I learn how to program in  art and how did the tidy verse help me  that since that's my talk so the short  story is that I basically just read  garret Grauman's and has Lee Wickham's  our for data science i sat there and I  had it pinned to my browser and I would  read a chapter and I would take notes  and then I would read it again and so so  that's pretty much how I learned but  there's a lot of sophisticated arguments  about non-standard evaluation and  whether the syntax in tidier  is better than it isn't besar and all  those things and I'm not going to talk  about that because as a beginner it  didn't really matter to me which way was  better it just mattered that there was a  way and tidy verse I think really helped  me and that it was nice and bounded and  you could do things with it but it was  also limited yes deep liars a huge  package but you don't need to know at  least at the beginning about most of  deep liar so I really enjoyed the  bounded nature of it  you read in data with read are you do  something with it with deep liar and  then you visualize it with ggplot and  you're like yes I have something so that  ability it showed me that okay one I can  learn to do this so that's good but to  that it would be valuable for me to put  in the effort to learn how to do this so  what really scared me I think at the  beginning of learning to code and our  was what one of the things that makes  are great which is all the packages  there's so many different ways to do all  these different things but I was not  interested in that and so at the  beginning what I really was doing was I  took solace in library tidy verse and  just type that in and then know that  everything will be okay  and so here I really have to thank the  entire our community our studio  community its high diverse community for  helping me learn for all the great  resources that there are for keeping me  excited about learning more I started a  blog where I  went through sort of my learning process  at Jessie Sadler calm and the response  that I've gotten from that sort of has  been way larger than I would have ever  expected in the first project that I was  working on the SF package came out right  or was really getting going right as I  was doing that so it was great I really  had fun delving into SF and into GIS and  so far I have a map of letters up to  1591 it's not the code that's holding me  back it's going through and cataloging  all those letters which is a laborious  when you have to actually input your own  data the coding seems easier at least it  takes less time okay so what I want to  do for the rest of the talk is to  discuss a new project that I've been  working on a package that I have built  that deals specifically with a historic  issue and so we're really going down to  narrow use case but hopefully you'll  bear with me here  so the package that I have developed  I've called Deb keeper which is short  for double entry bookkeeping or double  entry bookkeeping  which I know sounds amazing already so  this is on github right now but the  package is basically there's more I want  to do with but it's basically ready and  it has a package down website with three  vignettes and descriptions of how to do  things okay so let me start before I get  into what that is let me discuss what  the historical issue that I was seeking  to solve this so I'm interested in the  history of accounting and that might not  sound that interesting or you may be an  accountant I don't know but  the reason I'm interested in a history  of accounting is because accounting  deals with social relationships it's not  the accounting part it's the social  relationships and why accounting is  particularly interesting for me in my  context is that in the early modern  period in most of the Low Countries  where I do my work inheritance was  perfectly partible meaning that all male  and female heirs receive the exact same  amount of inheritance and when you're  dealing with merchant families that  means accounting has to go into figuring  out how much the estate is worth and  then how much each individual person  gets and it has to be exactly right so  and here you can see a document where  it's doing that so merchant families  inheritance involves accounting now one  problem with that other than its  accounting is that the currency that  they use as you can see on the right is  essentially pounds shillings and pence  one pound if you can't remember before  pre decimalisation equals 20 shillings  one shilling is equal to twelve pence so  this creates a number of problems  because it's not decimal firstly erect  arithmetic calculations are cumbersome I  never thought I would be doing so much  arithmetic as a historian so I have  pages and pages of just doing  handwritten arithmetic it's not hard but  more importantly probably is how to deal  with tripartite non decimal values in a  database and so this has the same issues  if you're gonna try and think about it  in our or any computer computers are  decimal and so firstly you have three  separate units  to make up one value the units have non  decimal bases and to make things worse  the bases can be different so 20 and 12  is the most usual but there's also 20  and 16 and there's 16 and 60 and 12 and  all sorts of different things okay so  how did I get over this in the def  keeper package I created a class that is  that I call LSD which comes from the  latin names for pounds shillings and  pence Libre solidus and Denarius so  you're getting some latin in here so  what does it do basically it makes these  values a numeric vector of length 3 it  also has a basis attribute that keeps  track of what the basis for the  shillings in pounds and pence are and  then they are stored as lists so that  you can use them as a list column and  you can have multiple in one object so  this is basically what it looks like  here from a vector Deb as LSD Deb as in  double entry bookkeeping and then you  can also input these values as separate  columns in a spreadsheet or something as  CSV and then bring them together with a  function called Deb LST gather so the  coolest thing that I first started off  with and you might not think this is  cool but it was really cool to me  one might say magical was that I could  normalize the values so I could add up  pounds add up shillings out of pence and  then get what that value actually is and  when I got this to work and it's just  pretty much arithmetic behind the scenes  it really was nice or you can add these  together within a function  okay so let's do some harder arithmetic  so we can compare the Deb keeper method  with the method from the third edition  of the encyclopedia encyclopaedia  britannica from 1797 so this is how they  actually talked about doing arithmetic  so this is in the arithmetic article so  here you can see on the left a fairly  complex way in which they multiply  fifteen pounds three shillings and eight  pence by 32 they break it down into  eight multiplied by 8 and then multiply  by four and they're doing they're  carrying over the unit's  sort of behind the scenes division is  even harder  so I'm gonna concentrate on there's two  examples here but the example on the  right is and here we get two different  bases so this is a weight so it's  dividing three hundred forty-five  hundred weight one quarter and eight  pounds by 22 and if you try and figure  out what they're doing here it's really  confusing and they have little dots to  figure out where they are but it's not  helpful and so we have to change our  bases and so that's what you see there  at the bottom of the the third line  there is that the bases are so a quarter  weight is there's four in a hundred  weight and there's 28 pounds in a  quarter I didn't know this either before  I looked at this so okay so it's nice  that we get to not have to do all this  I like 5th grade math I mean it I never  knew that 5th grade math would be so  important but but what about larger  account books so this is a picture of  the person whose account book I've  worked on the most  the account book itself young Dilla  fired outta or the elder so his account  book if you look at the inside 